<section id="shell">
<h2>Provider commands share a scripting tool.</h2>
<p>The model submits <code>bash({ script })</code>. The gateway interprets the script; installed provider command words dispatch to broker-hosted Wasm. Other model tools may also be configured—the whole interface is not limited to one tool.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Shell habit comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Shell habit</th>
<th scope="col">Dekopon behavior</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Variables and pipes</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior">Pipeline stages exchange bytes through bounded pipes, including trailing newlines. <code>echo hi | wc -c</code> returns <code>3</code>. Providers read and write byte streams; a downstream close stops a provider producer with status <code>141</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Loops, functions and conditionals</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior">Supported, along with <code>set -e</code>, <code>-u</code> and <code>-o pipefail</code>. The last pipeline stage keeps its assignments; non-final stages run from isolated scope snapshots. This is not complete Bash/POSIX compatibility.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Redirection</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior">Named in-memory buffers preserve exact bytes, including binary data; <code>cat</code> copies them. Piped input is consumed once: <code>echo x | { cat; cat; }</code> prints <code>x</code> once. No host paths or inherited process environment.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">JSON and text</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior"><code>jq</code> reads JSON bytes and emits one compact JSON result per line; <code>-r</code> emits strings without quotes, <code>-n</code> ignores stdin, and <code>-s</code> collects inputs within the value budget. <code>xargs</code> reads one item per line, not a JSON array.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Assignment</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior"><code>x=$(...)</code> strips trailing LF bytes and preserves an object or array when the trimmed capture starts with <code>{</code> or <code>[</code>. Scalars stay text. Unquoted expansions follow Bash-style <code>IFS</code> splitting; quote a capture to preserve spaces and internal newlines. Text expansion requires valid UTF-8.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Expansion</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior">Filesystem globbing and brace/tilde expansion stay literal. <code>IFS</code> splitting is implemented; an unset <code>IFS</code> separates on space, tab and newline, while an empty value disables splitting.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Rejected operations</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior">Process substitution, subshells, <code>eval</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>source</code> and <code>export</code> are rejected. Background jobs need an explicit route opt-in.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Progress notes</th>
<td data-label="Dekopon behavior"><code>progress "text" [--eta S]</code> is a reserved builtin: a no-op unless the route sets <code>progressNotes: true</code>, and refused at provider load if a component tries to declare the same word.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Start with <code>cap --list</code> to see session grants, <code>cap --describe &lt;id&gt;</code> for a capability, and an installed word’s <code>--help</code> for syntax. Provider <code>gh</code> is not the installed GitHub CLI. A provider command reads its own pipe or here-doc, never an enclosing compound’s input.</p>
</section>
<section id="workflow">
<h2>Read a PR and filter its JSON output.</h2>
<p><strong>Illustrative, read-only workflow.</strong> In an agent session, ask the model to submit this script through the <code>bash</code> tool. Replace <code>owner/repo</code> and <code>7</code>. It needs the <a href="https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon-provider-gh/releases/tag/v0.11.1">GitHub component pinned by this core release’s fixture ↗</a> implementing <code>provider@0.4.0</code>, the <code>gh.pull-request.read</code> session/Cedar grant, and broker-bound GET access to <code>api.github.com</code> with a credential allowed to read the repository.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustrative PR read script"><code>set -e
set -o pipefail
gh pr view 7 -R owner/repo --json title,state | jq '{title, state}'</code>
</pre>
<p>Expected shape: <code>{"title":"Fix the build","state":"OPEN"}</code>, with values from your PR. The explicit <code>--json</code> selects JSON output; without it the provider prints text rows. <code>jq</code> parses those JSON bytes and writes the projection with a newline. This performs one remote read and no write; the example was source-checked, not run against GitHub.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Failure comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Failure</th>
<th scope="col">Shell status</th>
<th scope="col">Meaning</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Unknown word or capability absent from the session</th>
<td data-label="Shell status">127</td>
<td data-label="Meaning">Nothing invokes that capability.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Broker refusal</th>
<td data-label="Shell status">126</td>
<td data-label="Meaning">Access was denied; do not treat it as an empty successful result.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Provider / host execution error</th>
<td data-label="Shell status">Nonzero</td>
<td data-label="Meaning">Provider failures keep their typed exit status; generic host failures use 1. Earlier effects may already have happened.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Invalid provider arguments</th>
<td data-label="Shell status">2</td>
<td data-label="Meaning">Read the installed command’s help.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Downstream reader closed</th>
<td data-label="Shell status">141</td>
<td data-label="Meaning">The provider’s consumer stopped reading, as a bounded <code>head</code> may do; this can end a producer early.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p><code>pipefail</code> keeps a failed read from becoming a successful empty filter. It can also report <code>141</code> after an intentional downstream close. Re-running a write is not deduplication or rollback. <a href="https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon-provider-gh/tree/v0.11.1">GitHub provider source ↗</a> · <a href="/guides/access/#policy">Separate write grants</a>
</p>
</section>
<section id="jobs">
<h2>Background jobs require <code>limits.jobTimeoutMs</code>.</h2>
<p>A route opts in by setting that deadline. Then <code>cmd &amp;</code> starts a job that can outlive its script and turn, prints its job number and sets <code>$!</code>. Use <code>jobs</code>, <code>wait %N</code> or <code>kill %N</code> to inspect, await or stop it. Without the opt-in, these operations fail with status 1.</p>
<p><code>sessions.maxJobs</code> defaults to 2 across the gateway and also bounds retained job rows. Jobs inherit a scope snapshot; their variables and buffers don’t return to the parent. Unawaited completion sends bounded text to the originating conversation, but a busy gateway may drop that notice: <code>jobs</code> is the fallback. Jobs disappear on restart and cannot receive chat assets or send attachments.</p>
<p>A completion notice can start another turn and another job. The route opt-in therefore permits continued work after the original turn; the person’s stop word cancels their running jobs in that conversation. A job started by a read-only probe stays read-only.</p>
<h3>Providers can request child scripts.</h3>
<p><code>dekopon:spawn/run@0.1.0</code> asks the gateway to run a nested script under the same person, agent and broker leg. Every child capability still passes Cedar and the same tree budgets; nesting grants no extra authority. One child may be live per provider invocation, and cancellation stops descendants.</p>
<p>Child stdout is bounded script text delivered at exit, not a live pass-through pipe. Waiting pauses the provider’s work clock, but the shared tree deadline keeps running. Inherited stdin shares the parent’s remaining bytes, so the parent must stop reading it after the spawn. <a href="/guides/provider-sdk/#interfaces">SDK child-script contract</a> · <a href="{{ source }}/docs/gatewayd.md#detached-jobs">Job configuration ↗</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="wakes">
<h2>Wakes can schedule one turn or watch a read-only condition.</h2>
<p>A route with <code>wakes: true</code> and gateway wake storage offers <code>wake</code>. A scheduled wake returns once after a delay; a watch runs a bounded probe script at its configured interval without a model in the polling loop. The initial probe establishes a baseline without waking. On later checks, exit 0 wakes the agent, exit 1 keeps watching, and an error wakes it with the failure and retires the watch.</p>
<p>Each probe gets a fresh broker leg that refuses writes. A woken turn gets fresh authorization for the original authenticated person and conversation; the model cannot choose a different recipient. Pending rows can survive restart, but a row removed just before dispatch can be lost. Saturated ticks are skipped rather than queued. This is not durable job delivery.</p>
<p><a href="{{ source }}/docs/gatewayd.md#wakes">Wake limits and storage ↗</a>. Native Mac chat needs neither wakes nor a guest VM.</p>
</section>
<section id="limits">
<h2>Limits apply at different layers.</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Layer comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Layer</th>
<th scope="col">What stops work</th>
<th scope="col">Boundary / caveat</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Interpreter</th>
<td data-label="What stops work">Step, recursion, retained-value/output and capability-call budgets; a shared tree deadline. Nested interpreters share the tree’s step and retained-value accounting.</td>
<td data-label="Boundary / caveat">Every <code>jq</code> stage uses a worker subprocess, killed and waited on at deadline or cancellation. Linux bounds its address space to startup size plus 256 MiB; macOS does not apply that cap. A crash fails that stage.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Wasm invocation</th>
<td data-label="What stops work">Fresh store and instance with fuel, memory and work-time bounds. Stdio backpressure and child waits park work time, not the tree deadline.</td>
<td data-label="Boundary / caveat">Compiled code may be reused. Wasmtime is in-process, not an OS isolation boundary.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Host effects</th>
<td data-label="What stops work">HTTP and storage constraints are checked by native services.</td>
<td data-label="Boundary / caveat">Completed external writes and direct storage writes are not rolled back by a later trap or timeout.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Recorded command arguments and text payloads are size-bounded, not redacted. Streaming stages also record byte counts and close reasons. Do not type secret values into scripts expecting them to be hidden. <a href="/guides/traces/#privacy">What operators can see</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="agents">
<h2>Skills guide the model; budgets bound its calls.</h2>
<p>An agent’s <code>spec.skills</code> mounts reviewed directories containing <code>SKILL.md</code> and optional resource files. The model sees names and descriptions, then uses <code>read_skill</code> to load instructions on demand. The gateway reads the files at catalog load; a session doesn’t open the host filesystem. Keep secrets out of skills: the model can read all of them, and <code>allowed-tools</code> is metadata, not an enforced grant.</p>
<p>The route can name a model explicitly. Otherwise, the agent’s <code>modelClass</code> selects the first matching configured model; no match fails startup. Gateway model credentials are separate from broker-held provider credentials.</p>
<p>Optional <code>metering.budgets</code> applies per-agent token limits using fixed, rolling, session or refilling-credit meters. Every applicable meter must admit a chat or guest inference call before it is sent. Background journal summaries are recorded but bypass those meters. The gateway reserves an estimate and settles actual usage afterward; a larger final charge can leave debt that blocks later calls. These are token limits, not a hard currency spending cap.</p>
<p>Budgets live in gateway memory. Optional <code>metering.restore</code> reads OpenObserve or Quickwit once at boot, best effort; missing telemetry can undercount earlier spend. Without a configured budget, token spend is recorded but unbounded. An optional guest model proxy shares these budgets and uses gateway model credentials; it doesn’t grant broker capabilities.</p>
<p><a href="{{ source }}/docs/catalog.md#skills-are-directories-the-model-reads-on-demand">Skill format ↗</a> · <a href="{{ source }}/docs/gatewayd.md#token-budgets">Model budget configuration ↗</a> · <a href="{{ source }}/docs/gatewayd.md#guest-model-proxy">Guest model proxy ↗</a>.</p>
</section>
<section id="assets">
<h2>References select files; handles bound access.</h2>
<figure class="ownership-map">
<div class="map-zone">
<h3>Gateway owns the conversation</h3>
<p><code>chat-asset:3</code> → conversation metadata + private upload spool</p>
<p>Optional model fetch sends image content to the model endpoint.</p>
</div>
<p class="flow-label">Unix socket / SCM_RIGHTS ↓ JSON metadata + read-only descriptors</p>
<div class="map-zone broker-zone">
<h3>Broker owns the host descriptors</h3>
<p>Wasm receives an opaque invocation-scoped handle, not a host path or OS descriptor.</p>
<p>Handle → bounded guest reads OR native streamed HTTP upload</p>
<p>Output writer → attach → read-only unlinked descriptor ↑ gateway</p>
</div>
<figcaption>Asset payloads stay out of broker JSON frames. Guest reads still copy bytes; model image requests still carry image data. This is not end-to-end zero-copy transport.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Pass <code>chat-asset:N</code> as its own unquoted provider argument. Quoted references and values expanded from variables are ordinary text, not attachment handles. <code>asset cat chat-asset:3 | head</code> streams text through a separately granted asset provider; builtins never open attachments directly.</p>
<p><strong>Attach is not send.</strong> Attach retains an output for the conversation; a separately granted <code>asset.send</code> operation delivers it. Failed invocations admit no asset effects. Queued deliveries and retained scratch bytes don’t survive restart. With a journal and separate asset store, eligible inbound attachment metadata, numbering and already-downloaded bytes can survive restart; generated outputs are not restored. Evicted inbound bytes are fetched again on use when the platform still has them. Closing the last descriptor releases an unlinked file.</p>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Asset budget comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Asset budget</th>
<th scope="col">Limit / accounting</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">One asset / one invocation</th>
<td data-label="Limit / accounting">8 MiB decoded per asset; 40 MiB cumulative decoded input or output. Repeated upload occurrences count again.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Host copy / descriptor count</th>
<td data-label="Limit / accounting">64 KiB per host call; at most five distinct input references and five attached outputs per invocation.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Conversation / delivery</th>
<td data-label="Limit / accounting">No per-conversation row cap for a live generation; at most four new sends per turn. A shared disk budget defaults to 1 GiB and counts stored bytes, separately from decoded-content ceilings. Journal retention owns durable inbound bytes; generated files stay session-only.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Asset-producing providers must implement the current asset and <code>provider@0.4.0</code> interfaces. Old base64 result envelopes are refused; rebuilding a provider against the current SDK does not make an old data-URL recipe a descriptor workflow. <a href="{{ source }}/docs/upgrading.md#asset-handles-0180">Provider migration notes ↗</a>.</p>
